{"t":1789706883,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"fdd17580-1e44-4052-8091-d9ad9214aa80","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-18T01:00:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 180100\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n900 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nWhile showers are spotty in parts of northeastern West Virginia\nat this time, additional showers and embedded thunderstorms have\nformed two linear segments across southwestern Pennsylvania and\nsoutheastern Pennsylvania. These segments have developed along a\ncold front. A cooler pattern still remains a possibility next\nweek.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Shower and thunderstorm chances shift southward with a cold\n  front through Friday, which will be followed by cooler\n  temperatures.\n\n- 2) Showers and thunderstorms return at times Sunday into next\n  week, with temperatures likely falling below normal.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Shower and thunderstorm chances shift southward\nwith a cold front through Friday, which will be followed by \ncooler temperatures.\n\nA few showers are noted in spots across parts of northeastern\nWest Virginia. While these showers should come and go over the\nnext hour or two, we will be watching two linear segments of\nshowers and embedded thunderstorms in southwestern and\nsoutheastern Pennsylvania. These segments have lost some of\ntheir lightning activity, but remain in tact enough that if they\nmove into Maryland in an hour or two, they could bring brief\ngusty winds and downpours. The thunderstorms are not severe.\nThis activity that we are watching are along a cold front that\nwill continue to push southward throughout the night and during\nthe day Friday. Some patchy fog could occur particularly in\nareas that have received rainfall this past afternoon and early\nevening. \n\nWhile the wind shift portion of the cold front likely pushes\nsouth of the area Friday morning, the moisture gradient may hang\nback across central Virginia up to the Potomac Highlands. As a \nshortwave trough swings through during the afternoon, some \nadditional showers and thunderstorms may develop in these areas.\nTemperatures will fall back into the lower to mid 80s with\nnortherly gusts up to 20 mph at times. There may also be a fair\namount of stratocumulus at times as well. A cooler night lies \nahead as high pressure crosses southern Quebec into New England.\nFriday night's lows should be in the upper 50s to mid 60s. \n\nHigh pressure to the northeast on Saturday should provide enough\ndry air to keep rain at bay. One exception may be along the\nAllegheny Front due to convergence in an inverted surface\ntrough. Warm advection aloft and mid level perturbations will \nlikely provide cloud cover areawide. Temperatures will be \ncloser to climatology, ranging from the mid 70s to lower 80s.\nLow pressure moving into the Ohio Valley may increase rain\nchances Saturday night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms return at times Sunday\ninto next week, with temperatures likely falling below normal.\n\nA cold front drops through the forecast area on Sunday before \nstalling to the south, bringing daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. In addition to daily precipitation, a cooler airmass \nbuilds overhead in the wake of the cold front, yielding cooling \ntemperatures each day. On Sunday, high temperatures will be in the \nlow 80s for most (70s mtns) with overnight lows in the 60s. By \nmidweek, high temperatures will top out in the upper 50s to 60s with \novernight lows in the 50s. These temperatures remain somewhat\nuncertain with large model spread, but the forecast favors the\ncooler side of guidance given a cold air damming surface\npattern. Overall, nearby disturbances bring unsettled weather \nto the region beginning Sunday and persisting through midweek.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIf patchy fog develops near the terminals tonight, it could drop\nvisibility to MVFR or IFR for an hour or two later tonight.\nOtherwise, a passing shower or embedded thunder could move\nthrough MRB or IAD the remainder of tonight. Additional showers\nand thunderstorms could develop as the front pushes south\nthrough the day Friday, but could mainly be near CHO terminal.\n\nOtherwise, VFR conditions are anticipated across terminals through \nmost of Friday. Patchy mountain valley fog could briefly bring \nVSBYs down at KMRB and KCHO to MVFR early Friday morning before \nreturning to VFR through most of the day. Winds shift northerly \nFriday morning and conditions remain largely unchanged \nthroughout the day Saturday. Beginning Sunday, more showers and \nthunderstorms could intermittently impact terminals as a trough \nmoves through the region with southerly winds potentially \ngusting to 20 kt.\n\nWinds shift to north/northeasterly on Monday in the wake of the\nfront, gusting 10 to 15 knots in the afternoon. Showers and \nthunderstorms are possible each day, with restrictions expected \nduring heaviest precipitation.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nIf the linear segment of showers and embedded thunder over\nsoutheastern Pennsylvania moves into northern zones of the\nChesapeake Bay late this evening into the overnight, it could\nprompt Marine Weather Statements or a Special Marine Warning.\nThe latter has a low chance of occurring. A Small Craft \nAdvisory remains in effect for the wider waters until 11 PM.\n\nWinds shift to northerly on Friday morning behind the cold\nfront. Some northerly channeling effects are possible on Friday\nmorning into the afternoon due to the frontal surge. Small Craft\nAdvisories may be needed for such an evolution, although current\nguidance does not offer enough confidence in more than\noccasional marginal gusts. A second surge could also occur\nFriday evening. As high pressure passes by to the north, winds \nshift from northeasterly to east- southeasterly on Saturday. \nThis could bring a few gusts to near 20 knots which could \napproach advisory levels, especially by Saturday night.\n\nSmall Craft Advisory conditions are possible on Sunday as \nsouth/southwest winds gust up to 20 knots across the waters. Winds \ndiminish overnight before shifting to north/northeasterly on Monday \nwith SCA criteria wind gusts possible. Additionally, showers and \nthunderstorms each day may lead to SMWs.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nWith winds remaining out of the south to southeasterly direction,\nthis flow regime has kept tidal anomalies around 0.50 to 0.75\nfeet. This has been enough to push a few of the more sensitive \nlocations into the Action stage. Until a cold front moves \nthrough early Friday, water levels will remain somewhat \nelevated. Although no tidal site is shown to experience any \ncoastal flooding, it will need to be monitored. This is \nparticularly the case at Annapolis, especially during the higher\nof the two astronomical high tides tonight. Expect anomalies to\ndrop on Friday into Saturday behind the mentioned cold front,\nthough anomalies may increase again due to southerly flow\nSaturday night into Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 11 PM EDT this evening for \n     ANZ532>534-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/AVS/KLW\nAVIATION...ADS/AVS/KLW\nMARINE...ADS/AVS/KLW\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789706852,"days":{"2026-09-18":{"hi":86,"lo":65,"pop":21,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-09-19":{"hi":81,"lo":68,"pop":9,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-20":{"hi":84,"lo":67,"pop":56,"short":"Showers And Thunderstorms Likely"},"2026-09-21":{"hi":78,"lo":64,"pop":53,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-22":{"hi":66,"lo":57,"pop":55,"short":"Rain Showers Likely"},"2026-09-23":{"hi":63,"lo":57,"pop":39,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-24":{"hi":66,"lo":nul